tes are not “members” for purposes of CNCL Section 5056.\nProcess and Role\nTo become an Associate, an application must be filed. The requirements to be an Associate are the same as those of Fellows, except that Associates do not need to pay full dues. Associates are bound by the same code of conduct as Fellows.\nAssociates may attend but not vote or participate in Council meetings, and may attend but not vote at Fellowship meetings. However, they may attend and participate in Working Group meetings, other meetings and seminars, and online forums at the Council\u0027s discretion.\nResignation\nAny Associate may resign or have their association terminated by the same process as Fellows, provided, however, that, at the discretion of the Council, Associates may not be given notice nor an opportunity to be heard by the Council before termination.\n   GOVERNING BODY\nCouncil role, size, and compensation\nThe Council is the governing body of RIIS 2.0, and its Councilors serve as the “board of directors” for purposes of CNCL Section 5210. It is responsible for the execution of the overall policy and direction of the association and may delegate responsibility for day-to-day operations to staff and working groups. The Council shall have up to fifteen (15), but no fewer than three (3) Fellows; the exact authorized number to be fixed, within these limits, by resolution of the Council.\nThe Council shall be empowered to appoint Officers with duties described in Section 6 of this Article.\nTerms\nElected Councilors will serve for 36 months, with the term beginning at the annual meeting during or immediately following their election. One-third (1/3) of the Council (at least 2 Councilors) shall be renewed every 12 months. Nominations for incoming Councilors shall take place within the twelve months prior to the upcoming annual meeting. Voting for nominated Councilors shall take place in the immediate 6 months prior to the upcoming annual meeting.\nThere is no term limit on serving as a Councilor.\nQualifications and Election Procedures\nAny Fellow may nominate another Fellow, who has been a Fellow for a minimum of a year or has played a leadership role in a comparable organization, as a candidate to the list of nominees for the Council. Nominees shall be natural persons of the age of majority in their home jurisdiction, such as state, province, or country of residence.\nNominations will be collected by a Volunteer Coordination Working Group.\nCouncil Elections\nCouncilors will be elected by the Fellows as specified in Policy and Procedures.\nElections shall be held annually or as otherwise specified in the Policies and Procedures. The Volunteer Coordination Working Group or the current Council, if this Working Group is not constituted or functioning, shall issue a call for Councilor candidates at least 120 days ahead of the elections. The Volunteer Coordination Working Group shall recruit nominations based on the representation considerations in the Belonging and Volunteer Coordination Policies and Procedures and publish a list of nominated candidates.\nElections are by a vote of the Fellows on all candidates nominated by the deadline. \nFor the first election, one-third of the Councilors shall hold office for 3 years, one-third for 2 years, and the remaining third for one year. If the total number of Councilors is not divisible by three, partition into thirds will be done approximately, allocating more persons to the shorter terms; for example, if the total number is 11, the partition shall be 4 persons for one year, 4 persons for two years, and 3 persons for three years. Thereafter, each newly elected Councilor shall serve 3 years. Elections shall be by secret ballot and conducted securely online.\n"},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":12709,"ei":12709,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.1500000000000001,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_il_i":false,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":true,"ps_ifl":36.0,"ps_