y Councilor present at a meeting duly held at which a quorum is present. Councilors present at a meeting at which a matter is being decided shall be given the opportunity to ask questions and voice opinions. After all questions have been answered, the individual facilitating the meeting will ask if there are objections. A Councilor may object to a decision on the grounds that it violates or may violate a fiduciary duty, will result in irreparable harm to the Corporation, or is inconsistent with the mission of the Corporation. A decision will be adopted if no Councilors object to it. If a consensus is not achieved or the Council decides via the Consensus Decision Making procedure to vote, then that act or decision should be done via a vote as per Section 5.\nVoting\nAny act taken or decision made by a vote of the majority of the Councilors present at a meeting duly held at which a quorum is present is the act of the Council, unless a greater number is expressly required by the CNCL, the Articles of Incorporation, or these Bylaws. For avoidance of doubt, the following actions shall require a vote by a majority of all Councilors then in office, rather than simply a majority of the quorum, in order to be effective: \nApproval of contracts or transactions in which a Councilor has a direct or indirect material financial interest (provided that the vote of any interested Councilor(s) is not counted); \nCreation of, and appointment to, committees exercising the authority of the Council; and \nRemoval of a Councilor for noncompliance with the qualifications set forth in these Bylaws as described in Article VII, Section 8 (provided that only the vote of qualified councilors is counted).\nAction without Meeting\nAny action permitted to be taken at a meeting of the Council may be taken without a meeting if all members of the Council entitled to vote thereon consent in writing to the action and the number of Councilors then in office constitutes a quorum. \nConduct of Meetings\nMeetings of the Council shall be presided over by the Chair of the Council, or, in their absence, by an Officer designated by a majority of the Councilors present at the meeting. The Secretary shall act as secretary of all meetings of the Council, provided that, in their absence, the presiding officer shall appoint another person to act as the secretary of the meeting. Meetings shall follow the Policies and Procedures of RIIS 2.0 \n\u000b\u000bWORKING GROUPS\nTypes of Working Groups\nThere will be several standing Working Groups. Initially, these will include Finance, Infrastructure, Affiliation, Volunteer Coordination, and Communications. Standing Working Groups shall include at least one Councilor; however, the Council will have discretion to relax this requirement for specific Working Groups if needed. \nThe Council may also create ad-hoc Working Groups and appoint their chairpersons and Council liaisons as needed. Ad-hoc Working Groups must be chaired by a Fellow. For avoidance of doubt, Working Groups are not “committees exercising the authority of the board” for purposes of CNCL Section 5212. \nMeetings and Action of Working Groups\nEach Working Group’s role is to support the Council by providing expertise, ensuring efficient management of specific functions (outlined by each Working Group’s remit), and providing recommendations to the Council on major decisions needed, while the Council makes the final decisions and maintains overall oversight of the organization. See RIIS 2.0 Policies and Procedures. Each Working Group will have a set of responsibilities outlined by the Council in its Policies and Procedures, and may perform these responsibilities without Council approval, but must keep the Council apprised of activities via meeting notes or via the Councilor serving on the Working Group.\n"},{"ty":"as","st":"list","si":25888,"ei":25888,"sm":{"ls_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":true,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"t